Lenses Bug Report: src/henchman/agents/working_memory.py\n## Date: 
2025-03-13T15:00:00Z\n## Bug Summary\nThe `should_read_file` method incorrectly 
returns `True` for files that were previously read fully (`lines_read="full"`). 
When a file has been read fully, any subsequent read (even with line ranges) 
should return `False` since the entire file content is already in memory.\n\n## 
Lens\n**Phase 1: The Intent vs. Reality Check** - Logic Gap\n\n## Expected vs. 
Observed\n| Expected Behavior | Observed Risk |\n|---|---|\n| When 
`lines_read="full"`, any subsequent `should_read_file` call should return 
`False` (file already read) | The method returns `True` because 
`LineRange.from_str("full")` returns `None`, causing the overlap check to fail 
|\n| The system should prevent redundant file reads when entire file is already 
cached | Agents will waste time and tokens re-reading files that are already 
fully cached |\n\n## Root Cause\nThe `LineRange.from_str("full")` returns `None`
because "full" doesn\'t match the "start-end" format. The `should_read_file` 
method checks `if rng and rng.overlaps(...)`, which evaluates to `False` when 
`rng` is `None`, causing it to fall through to the `return True` case.\n\n## Fix
Plan\nModify the `should_read_file` method to handle the special case where 
`match.lines_read == "full"`. When a file was read fully, we should return 
`False` regardless of the requested line range.\n\n## Status: IN PROGRESS'})
